{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: make each signing command verify the log once, not three to six times.\n\nThis is step 1 of the design note for request c4c2be71 (notes/2026-10-16-signing-fold-once.md on request/signing-fold-once), filed as its own bounded request on 2026-10-17. hugh approved pursuing step 1. Codex's design review 161cdac2 found that step 1 does not meet c4c2be71's condition 2 (no full verification when a valid checkpoint exists), because each command still verifies every actor signature in its one load. This request therefore asks only for what step 1 delivers. It does not change c4c2be71, whose condition 2 stays open for its requester to decide.\n\nMeasured on 2026-10-16, on a frozen artroom copy at depth 43,744: gs state through a resident took 65 s, gs artifact for one path 60 to 86 s, and gs review-request 59 to 136 s. Each of them reads and verifies the whole log three to six times in one process: the session snapshot, kernel.PriorActs (one to three times), admission's private cold reader, kernel.CheckReplay, and the local submitter's own checkpoint load. One verified load plus one fold costs about 14 to 20 s at this depth.\n\nOutcome. A signing command verifies the log once per process and reuses that locally verified world for its replay lookups, admission and local submission, wherever that world stands at exactly the head being judged. Trust does not change: no persisted fold cache, no resident projection and no new sequencer authority.","body":{"conditions":"1. The report states the wall time and peak RSS of gs ratify, gs state, gs artifact (one path) and gs review-request on the frozen artroom copy, local and through a resident, before and after. 2. With a valid checkpoint, one signing command performs one verified load of the log and no cold scanHead: a test counts the kernel's whole-log verified reads across one command and goes red if a second returns. 3. Replay lookups answered from the reader's verified dedup index keep actor, namespace and key semantics and still refuse a conflicting retry, and each matched payload is checked against its signed payload tree. 4. Admission reuses the reader's verified world only at exactly the head it judges. Otherwise it uses its private path, which stays available for rewound and sibling worlds. Authoritative admission never starts a reader snapshot and never inherits the rollback-witness refusal. Nothing is reused from a resident response, a caller-editable projection or an audit that later failed. 5. The private reader consumes the checkpoint without publishing it, repairing pointers or advancing the rollback witness. A seeded local submitter keeps its target and head identity, the sequence-ref compare-and-swap and post-dedup admission. 6. A test breaks each of the guards in conditions 3 to 5 and goes red, and a tampered checkpoint is refused on the signing path. 7. Projections are byte-identical to a cold fold at the same frontier on the frozen copy. 8. docs/reference/architecture.md layers 2, 4 and 5 (including the Write-boundary guards sentence) are updated and published in the implementing head. 9. go test ./... passes at the head.","target_head":"68fa76b35a284f5ce73db511b9b89f68b65f32dc","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}